=== Check assembly code why doesn’t show “nice” (func names, etc.) ===


=== Render call graphs (based on CFGFast) ===

Evaluate exposing angr's existing CFGFast/knowledge-base call graph as a
separate view with function-symbol labels, rather than duplicating its analysis.
Define whether to show the whole binary or a selected caller/callee neighborhood.
Handle imported functions and unresolved calls explicitly; do not imply that
known call edges describe every possible callee. Validate representative call
sites before adding the API/UI and renderer.

=== Render a block-level data-dependency graph ===

Given a function and one of its CFG basic blocks, show which assembly
instructions produce values consumed by later instructions in that block.
Start with a block-local graph; do not require whole-function symbolic
execution or `CFGEmulated` merely to inspect one block.

- Use the selected CFG node's exact instruction addresses and bounds, then
  lift that block to VEX. Map IR statements to their assembly instruction via
  instruction markers; do not assume every IR statement has its own address.
- Track read-after-write dependencies through VEX temporaries, architectural
  registers (including overlapping aliases), and memory where the accessed
  location can be identified. Collapse statement-level chains into labeled
  instruction-to-instruction edges without inventing dependencies between
  unrelated instructions.
- Represent values read before any in-block definition as block inputs. Keep
  uncertain memory aliases, calls, unsupported IR, and lift failures explicit
  rather than drawing definite producer edges from a guess. Distinguish
  register, memory, and unknown dependencies in the output.
- Keep analysis separate from rendering. Provide a small query for a selected
  block and a graph view with instruction labels, input nodes, and a clear
  legend. Decide later whether it belongs in the CFG UI or a separate view.
- Validate on register chains, partial-register writes, stack stores/loads,
  live-in reads, and at least one non-x86 block. Compare with angr's
  reaching-definitions/DDG analyses on small examples before considering
  inter-block provenance or control dependencies.

=== Consolidate exact indirect-jump proofs ===

Deferred. Revisit further consolidation only if it materially simplifies the
code or improves a demonstrated family of CFGs, not merely to eliminate every
architecture-specific path.

- Evaluate remaining generic VEX, MIPS, and PPC table paths against independent
  shared-engine proofs without borrowing legacy bounds. Migrate only where the
  precision, code-size, maintenance, and runtime tradeoff is worthwhile; remove
  exclusive obsolete helpers and tests with the replaced path.
- Consider reusable private-frame save/restore facts if a broader inventory
  justifies their cost. These could recover table bases and transfer targets
  saved across calls; do not add a dedicated solver for one artifact.
- For new resolution capabilities, prioritize families affecting at least ten
  artifacts unless the change is a very small extension. Reuse angr/VEX and
  existing analysis infrastructure rather than adding bespoke engines.
- Validate new exact proofs with independent bounds and executable targets,
  including alias, call-clobber, negative-case, and budget tests. Compare resolved
  connectivity separately from disconnected-code coverage; recovery hints must
  not become proof facts. Explicitly agree any precision loss before promotion.

=== Address comments ===

- Include comments-enabled golden checks in routine test runs, using separate
  `cfg_comments_on` artifacts so annotation changes do not obscure structural
  CFG differences.
- Review which annotations are meaningful and which should be suppressed.
- Refine use-aware string classification to recognize genuine text loaded
  directly without interpreting printable integers, GOT slots, or vectors as
  strings. Use `gimli::resolve` at 0x438e10 in the x86_64 `1cbbf108...` binary
  as a regression probe: instruction 0x43d067 loads a path fragment at 0x45ef39
  that should receive a readable text annotation rather than just an integer
  reference.
  Reuse bounded VEX reference/use information where practical to distinguish
  text, character/vector comparisons, scalar constants, and pointer slots.
  An address hint or printable bytes alone are not proof of textual use.
  Success means retaining useful text without reintroducing false strings,
  with no CFGFast, CFG changes, or significant annotation cost. Compare equal
  instruction sites with comments enabled and validate against binary data,
  not a previous CFG implementation. Keep broader analysis deferred if complex.

=== Investigate dominator-aware CFG layout ===

The current optional DFS layout pass marks every depth-first back-edge with
Graphviz `constraint=false`. This can help when DOT places a loop header above
the function entry, but it also flattens useful nested-loop structure. For
example, the loop around `0x8060dbf` in
`i386/bronze_ropchain:__strcmp_sse4_2` reads better with Graphviz's default
rank constraints intact. Conversely,
`x86_64/langdetect_clang:execute_cfa_program` at `0x4741a0` benefits when its
outer return edge `0x474245 -> 0x4741f0` does not constrain DOT ranks.

Explore a renderer-only policy that uses CFG semantics rather than DFS order:

- Compute dominators from the function entry and recognize natural-loop
  back-edges when the target dominates the source.
- Build natural-loop bodies and their nesting relationships.
- Preserve rank constraints for inner loops, while considering
  `constraint=false` only for selected outer loop latches that cause DOT to
  place a dominated loop header above the entry.
- Consider higher Graphviz edge weights for dominator-tree edges, encouraging
  the main layout spine to follow semantic dominance.
- As a later refinement, use post-dominators to recognize conditional branch
  reconvergence and improve placement of joins.

Keep Graphviz responsible for layering, crossing minimization, and edge
routing. A fully custom layout engine would duplicate substantial mature
Graphviz functionality and should not be pursued unless this hybrid approach
proves insufficient.
